73468138230965e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.0967194612021558e-05,-4.529942543740617e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,49454,323,14927,1817,1744,315,264,79151,47311,13,2014,862,12761,11,2176,29136,28370,1191,400,87,61,17,10,19,87,41715,4385,2412,12947,576,11341,315,279,49319,517,11,400,65,61,17,12,19,580,54876,315,16247,594,47311,311,279,49319,517,315,14927,594,47311,374,6144,311,279,11341,315,14927,594,6783,4647,311,16247,594,6783,4647,13,1416,862,6783,3793,525,537,6144,11,1477,279,2629,315,279,6783,3793,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nEric and Charles each think of a quadratic polynomial. To their surprise, both quadratics start $x^2+4x+\\cdots$. The ratio of the discriminant, $b^2-4ac$, of Eric's polynomial to the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. If their constant terms are not equal, find the sum of the constant terms.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"54ed9d3dc559964fe71eb98c1e0fc2f66cd8d899a9b66bfe4ebf42deac99ab59"},"seconds":29.756821393966675,"seed":569291319002421912,"stop":"eos","text":"Let the quadratic polynomials be:\n- Eric's polynomial: \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\)\n- Charles's polynomial: \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\)\n\nThe discriminant of a quadratic polynomial \\(x^2 + bx + c\\) is given by \\(b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nFor Eric's polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\):\n- The discriminant is \\(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot a = 16 - 4a\\).\n\nFor Charles's polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\):\n- The discriminant is \\(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot b = 16 - 4b\\).\n\nAccording to the problem, the ratio of the discriminants of Eric's polynomial to Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. This can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{4(4 - a)}{4(4 - b)} = \\frac{4 - a}{4 - b}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 - a}{4 - b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to eliminate the fractions, we get:\n\\[\na(4 - a) = b(4 - b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\\[\n4a - a^2 = 4b - b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[\na^2 - b^2 = 4a - 4b\n\\]\n\nFactoring both sides:\n\\[\n(a - b)(a + b) = 4(a - b)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a \\neq b\\), we can divide both sides by \\(a - b\\):\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the constant terms \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Let the quadratic polynomials be:\n- Eric's polynomial: \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\)\n- Charles's polynomial: \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\)\n\nThe discriminant of a quadratic polynomial \\(x^2 + bx + c\\) is given by \\(b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nFor Eric's polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\):\n- The discriminant is \\(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot a = 16 - 4a\\).\n\nFor Charles's polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\):\n- The discriminant is \\(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot b = 16 - 4b\\).\n\nAccording to the problem, the ratio of the discriminants of Eric's polynomial to Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. This can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{4(4 - a)}{4(4 - b)} = \\frac{4 - a}{4 - b}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 - a}{4 - b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to eliminate the fractions, we get:\n\\[\na(4 - a) = b(4 - b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\\[\n4a - a^2 = 4b - b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[\na^2 - b^2 = 4a - 4b\n\\]\n\nFactoring both sides:\n\\[\n(a - b)(a + b) = 4(a - b)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a \\neq b\\), we can divide both sides by \\(a - b\\):\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the constant terms \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]","id":"algebra:1149"}
